speechtext:
The member for North
Perth (Mr. Morphy) said that he had heard no reason why the amendment should not go through. It is for the mover of an amendment to make good his case, and in the remarks which the member for West Toronto (Mir. Hocken) has made I see no reason why the amendment should be accepted. !He suggests that the Acting Minister of Finance has proved the worth of the amendment by saying that, if it were accepted, it would cost the Government a lot of money to carry it out. I do not think that that proved the case at all. Investigation may be made whether an applicant has a relative in the Senate, the House of Commons or the Civil Service, and it may be shown that he has none;

without. the services of a large staff of inbut in every case a special staff of inquisitors will be required. It has been pointed out that if you rely on the statement of the applicant as to his family connections, you open the door to his giving false information. In other words, the making of this provision would be detrimental to the country, first, because of the expenditure involved in carrying it out, and, second, (because of your opening the door to a man's .avoiding the truth in order to further his own ends. It would give members of this 'House a chance to vent grievances they might have against other members or to hold other members up to the country as having come into the House for the purpose of getting their relatives into the Civil Service. I can speak on this subject quite freely, because I have not a relative, either near or distant, in the Civil Service. I do hope, for the sake of my relatives, that they never will be in the Civil Service. I do not say that as a disparagement to any one. We want civil servants, and we want good ones, but they should not be subjected to the criticism of members of Parliament because they happen to be related to members of the Civil Service Commission, deputy ministers, or members of this House or the Senate. It seems to me that the only object of this motion is to give to those who desire to obtain political advantage the chance to come into the House and say: So-and-so has been abusing his powers, So-and-so has come here solely for the purpose of getting members of his family into the Service. 1 agree with all that has been said by the member for Shelburne and Queens. This legislation is either a camouflage or the basis of a real law. I think it is to be a real law, and that everybody in the House is endeavouring to do the best he can to make it a gOud law, in the short time that we have at our disposal. I look upon this motion as a reflection on the motives of members of this House. It suggests that gentlemen come here with no other purpose than that of getting their relatives into the Civi1 Service, and if onlv for the sake of the dignity of the House, I shall oppose the motion.
